<article class="glass-card rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-8 text-slate-300">
  <div>
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-white tracking-tight mb-3">
      Break-Even Analysis & Revenue Threshold Guide
    </h2>
    <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
      A Break-Even Analysis identifies the exact sales volume or billable revenue your business must generate to cover all fixed and variable costs. Crossing your break-even point marks the boundary where your business stops operating at a loss and begins generating net profitability.
    </p>
  </div>

  {/* Formula Box */}
  <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800 space-y-3">
    <h3 class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-teal-400">
      Break-Even Point Formulas
    </h3>
    <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-teal-950/40 border border-teal-500/30 text-center font-mono text-sm sm:text-base text-white font-bold space-y-1">
      <div>Break-Even Units = Total Fixed Costs / (Price Per Unit - Variable Cost Per Unit)</div>
      <div class="text-xs text-teal-300">Contribution Margin Ratio = (Price Per Unit - Variable Cost Per Unit) / Price Per Unit</div>
      <div class="text-xs text-teal-300">Break-Even Revenue = Total Fixed Costs / Contribution Margin Ratio</div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
      Fixed costs include rent, insurance, and baseline software retainers. Variable costs include materials, per-unit manufacturing costs, payment processor transaction fees, and shipping.
    </p>
  </div>

  {/* Worked Numerical Example */}
  <div class="space-y-4">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white">
      Worked Numerical Example: SaaS & Productized Service
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
      Suppose a productized agency offers a monthly SEO audit subscription package for $500 per client:
    </p>

    <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800 space-y-2 text-xs font-mono">
      <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-400"><span>1. Monthly Fixed Overhead (Office, Software, Salaries):</span><span class="text-slate-200">$6,000.00 / month</span></div>
      <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-400"><span>2. Selling Price Per Subscription Package:</span><span class="text-slate-200">$500.00</span></div>
      <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-400"><span>3. Variable Cost Per Subscription (Contractor payout, API credits):</span><span class="text-slate-200">$200.00</span></div>
      <div class="flex justify-between text-slate-400"><span>4. Unit Contribution Margin ($500 - $200):</span><span class="text-teal-300">$300.00 per client</span></div>
      <div class="flex justify-between text-white font-bold border-t border-slate-800 pt-1.5 text-sm"><span>Break-Even Volume ($6,000 / $300):</span><span class="text-teal-400">20 Clients / month</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-teal-950/30 border border-teal-500/30 text-xs text-slate-200 font-mono font-bold flex justify-between">
      <span>Break-Even Monthly Revenue:</span>
      <span class="text-teal-400 text-sm">$10,000.00 / month</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  {/* Practical Freelancer Advice */}
  <div class="space-y-3">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white">
      Strategic Decision Making Using Break-Even Analysis
    </h3>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
      <li><strong>Lower Your Fixed Expenses First:</strong> Auditing unused software subscriptions lowers your break-even threshold immediately, reducing business vulnerability during slow months.</li>
      <li><strong>Increase Unit Contribution Margin:</strong> Raising package prices or negotiating lower per-unit material costs expands your margin, requiring fewer client sales to achieve profitability.</li>
      <li><strong>Plan Capital Investments Safely:</strong> Before taking on a new $1,000/month office lease, use break-even analysis to see how many extra clients you must sign each month to pay for it.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  {/* Frequently Asked Questions */}
  <div class="space-y-4 pt-4 border-t border-slate-800">
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white">
      Frequently Asked Questions About Break-Even Analysis
    </h3>
    <div class="space-y-4 text-sm">
      <div class="border-b border-slate-800/80 pb-3">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-white mb-1">What is a contribution margin?</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-400">Contribution margin is the selling price minus variable costs per unit. It represents the portion of sales revenue that contributes to paying off fixed overhead expenses.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="border-b border-slate-800/80 pb-3">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-white mb-1">What happens when sales fall below the break-even point?</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-400">When sales volume is below break-even, total revenue is insufficient to cover fixed costs, resulting in a net financial loss for that accounting period.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="border-b border-slate-800/80 pb-3">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-white mb-1">Can freelancers use break-even analysis?</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-400">Yes! Freelancers can treat their living expenses and business software costs as fixed overhead to calculate how many client hours or projects they must bill each month to break even.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="border-b border-slate-800/80 pb-3">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-white mb-1">How often should a business recalculate break-even metrics?</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-400">Recalculate your break-even point whenever fixed costs change (e.g. hiring staff, renting an office) or when supplier prices shift.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pb-1">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-white mb-1">Are calculations saved online?</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-400">No. SimpleInvoiceTools executes all financial formulas 100% locally in your browser with zero remote logging.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</article>
